<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-bottom: 2px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 11th Grade (CEFR: B1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Intelligent Robots</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What is Artificial Intelligence?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Interpret instructions for more complex technology like using an application or a computer program. Interactive: Facilitate discussions on future career paths.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can interpret and explain instructions on using a professional networking site (e.g., "Some steps include choosing a platform, creating an account, and entering your experience, such as internships in tech hubs in Panama like Ciudad del Saber.")
Can facilitate and lead a group discussion on career interests, guiding classmates to share their goals (e.g., "What skills do you think are important for a career in technology?")
Can interpret classmates' career-related feedback during discussions on job skills (e.g., "How would you improve your teamwork skills?")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of various `intelligent robots` or `AI systems` (e.g., robotic arms in factories, self-driving cars, voice assistants). Students, in pairs, briefly discuss what they think `artificial intelligence` is and how it impacts `society`. Teacher guides brainstorming for initial ideas and key vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of key vocabulary for the lesson, focusing on the long vowel sound /ɑː/ in words like "robot," "job," and "start," and the alternation of full and reduced vowels in multisyllabic words such as "innovative" /ˈɪn.ə.veɪ.tɪv/ and "technological" /ˌtek.nəˈlɒdʒ.ɪ.kəl/. The teacher then models simple sentences using `future simple` to make predictions about `technology` (e.g., "AI will create new `opportunities` for `employment`.") and `conditional forms` (e.g., "If AI continues to `evolve`, it might `revolutionize` `job markets`.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'artificial,' is it natural or human-made?" (Expected answer: "Human-made.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When we say `technology` will 'evolve,' does that mean it will stay the same or change and develop over time?" (Expected answer: "Change and develop.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 0-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> An audio clip (e.g., a short informative podcast segment or expert interview) discussing the definition of `artificial intelligence`, its basic `functionality`, and its potential impact on `future` `job markets`. The register is semi-formal and informative, targeting B1.2 comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interpret receptive instructions for complex `technology` and understand basic explanations of `AI systems` and their `algorithms`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listen (Gist):</b> Students listen to the audio once and answer the question: "What is the main topic of the discussion, and what two general areas of life will `AI` most significantly `revolutionize`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listen (Specific Details):</b> Students listen again and complete True/False statements based on the audio content. Examples:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"AI `systems` are `programmed` to learn from `data` and perform `tasks`." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The speaker believes `AI` will completely eliminate all `employment opportunities`." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"New `innovative` `software` is being `developed` to `enhance` `customer service`." (True)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 10-20 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpret `guidelines` and `instructions` related to `technological` `systems`. Focus on `conditional forms` (e.g., "If AI continues to `evolve`...") `future simple` (e.g., "AI will `create`..."), `passive voice` (e.g., "`AI systems` are `programmed`..."), and target vocabulary including `artificial intelligence`, `robots`, `technology`, `society`, `opportunities`, `challenges`, `job markets`, `employment`, `algorithms`, `data`, `software`, `functionality`, `systems`, `advance`, `evolve`, `revolutionize`, `adapt`, `create`, `enhance`, `program`, `intelligent`, `complex`, `societal`, `significant`, `evolving`.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listening Cloze):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences from the audio, pausing at intervals. Students listen and fill in the blanks on a worksheet with missing target vocabulary words (e.g., "AI `systems` are ____ to learn from `data`." - *programmed*; "If `technology` continues to ____, it will bring both `opportunities` and ____." - *evolve, challenges*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion with Options):</b> Students listen to incomplete sentences related to `AI` and choose the best word or phrase from a given list that fits grammatically and semantically, using `conditional forms` or `future simple`. E.g., "If `AI` ____ (advances/advanced), it ____ (will change/would change) our `job markets` drastically." They listen to the complete sentence from the audio to check.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Checking & Pronunciation Drill:</b> Students work in pairs to review their answers, clarifying vocabulary and grammar. The teacher then leads a choral repetition of specific target words and phrases from the audio, especially focusing on the /ɑː/ sound in "robot" and "job," and the pronunciation of "innovative" and "technological."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 20-35 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Intelligent Robots".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: Group Discussion on AI and Future Careers</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4). The teacher provides a scenario prompt: "Imagine a new `intelligent` `AI system` is being `integrated` into `customer service` in Panama, capable of providing `personalized` responses and `updates`. Listen to these potential `guidelines` for its `functionality`. Discuss: `How will AI change the job market` in this sector? What new `employment opportunities` could it `create`, and what `challenges` might it present for existing `careers`? What `skills` do you think are important for a `career` in `technology` in an `evolving` `society` like Panama?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students listen to the prompt and `facilitate and lead a group discussion on career interests`, guiding classmates to share their `goals` and `interpret classmates' career-related feedback during discussions on job skills` (e.g., "How would you `improve` your `teamwork skills` for a tech `job`?"). They are encouraged to use `conditional forms`, `future simple`, `passive voice`, and `societal` `roles` of `AI` during their discussion.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 35-45 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly shares one key `opportunity` and one key `challenge` for `careers` related to `AI` in Panama, based on their discussion. They also explain one instruction related to complex `technology` they heard during the discussion (e.g., how an `AI system` `functions` or specific `guidelines` for using `software`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation/Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of target vocabulary (e.g., `robot`, `job`, `innovative`, `technological`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words related to `AI` and `careers`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity in interpreting and explaining instructions about `technology` and `AI functionality`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective collaboration and active listening during group discussions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of `conditional forms` and `future simple` in predictions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete 1-2 reflective prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was the most challenging part of listening to explanations or discussions about `AI` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How well do I feel I understood the main `opportunities` and `challenges` `AI` might bring to `job markets`?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: 45-50 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new vocabulary related to `AI` and `technology` did I learn today, and how can I use these words to talk about `future` `careers`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did listening to my classmates' predictions and ideas help me `adapt` my own understanding of `AI`'s `societal` `impact`?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today's lesson successfully introduced core `AI` vocabulary and grammar through listening tasks. Students engaged well with the audio and were able to identify main ideas and specific details. The group discussion in the production stage allowed them to practice `interactive listening` and `facilitate discussions` about `career paths`, using target language. A working point for next time might be to provide more explicit sentence starters for the `conditional forms` in the production phase to ensure wider usage. The pronunciation focus on /ɑː/ and multisyllabic words was generally well-received, but some students will need continued practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Next Lesson:</b> Today, we focused on understanding what `AI` is and its potential `impact` through listening. Next lesson, we will `read` a text that describes specific applications of `AI` in various `job markets` and how it continues to `advance` `technology`, preparing us to `interpret instructions` from written `guidelines` for `innovative` `systems`.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Day 1: End</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>